      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rockford%2C_Tennessee"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In the 1900s, the Rockford Manufacturing Company ran a cordage factory powered by a low‑head dam on the Little River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rockford%2C_Tennessee"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town hosts Tennessee’s only single‑room schoolhouse, the Sam Houston Historic Schoolhouse, a relic of early education.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.smokymountains.org/explore-the-area/rockford/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Rockford sits in northern Blount County, with the Little River flowing through it before joining the Tennessee River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rockford%2C_Tennessee"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Rockford incorporated in the early 1970s to keep from being annexed by neighboring Alcoa or Maryville.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rockford%2C_Tennessee"
    },
    {
